  "result": "**In Praise of Embers**\n\nFlames get all the glory. They leap, they roar, they demand to be watched. A campfire at its wildest, a birthday candle, a forest fire \u2014 we point and say *look at that*.\n\nEmbers are quieter. They don't perform. Long after the spectacle is over, after the logs have collapsed and the noise has died down, an ember just sits there in the ash, orange and stubborn and warm. It doesn't need an audience. It just refuses to go out.\n\nI've been thinking lately that most of the good things in a life are ember-work, not flame-work.\n\nA friendship that survives for twenty years isn't usually kept alive by grand gestures. It's kept alive by the small, unremarkable act of texting \"thinking of you\" six months later, for no reason at all. By remembering how someone takes their coffee. By showing up, again and again, in ordinary ways.\n\nA hobby that lasts isn't the one you burn hot for a month and abandon. It's the one you return to in fits and starts \u2014 the guitar in the corner, the half-finished novel in a drawer, the plant on the windowsill that you water just enough. You tend it a little. It stays.\n\nEven hope, I think, is more ember than flame. Flame hope is loud and cinematic: everything will be perfect tomorrow! Ember hope is different. It's making tea properly on a Tuesday. It's going for a walk when you don't really want to. It's planting something you know you won't see bloom until next spring. It's small and persistent and a little bit defiant.\n\nThere is a particular kind of warmth that only an ember can give. It's not the sudden heat of a fire to your face, but the lasting warmth you feel when you hold your hands over it for a long time. The kind that gets into your bones. The kind you carry with you when you walk back inside.\n\nSo I guess this is my small argument for tending the quiet things. Keep the ember going. Cup your hands around it. Don't worry if no one else can see the light. It's not for them.\n\nIf you have one \u2014 a friendship, a project, a weird little ritual that keeps you going \u2014 hold onto it carefully. It's probably the thing that will still be warm long after the fireworks are over.\n\nWhat's your ember?",
